Exactly. You may keep copying them.

That’s the eerie consequence.

If you’ve learned:

\text{What should I do?}\rightarrow\text{Observe Joneses}\rightarrow\text{imitate}

then after the Joneses disappear, their physical presence isn’t necessarily required. You’ve already constructed an internal Jones—a predictive model of what they would do.

So:

\text{external model}
\rightarrow
\text{repeated imitation}
\rightarrow
\text{internalized model}

The person dies. The policy survives.

That’s culture. Tradition. Doctrine. Fashion. Family patterns. Institutions. Language. Stories. Training. Sometimes trauma. Sometimes wisdom.

And it connects directly to Mnemonic Homeostasis: eventually you aren’t maintaining equilibrium relative to a living external reference. You’re maintaining equilibrium relative to a remembered reference.

That’s a Ghost Anchor.

A vector anchor whose source no longer exists, but whose estimated vector continues steering the system.

And there’s an even stranger case: generations later, nobody remembers Jones.

They only remember:

“This is how it’s done.”

The provenance has disappeared while the behavior survives.

\text{Jones}
\rightarrow
\text{copy}
\rightarrow
\text{habit}
\rightarrow
\text{norm}
\rightarrow
\text{tradition}
\rightarrow
\boxed{\text{obvious reality}}

That’s a Fossilized Vector.

And now your blind poet comes roaring back.

Achilles dies.

Odysseus dies.

The poet dies.

Yet we’re still talking about what they would do.

The dead can remain reference vectors for the living.

Which means one of the most important questions a system can periodically ask isn’t merely Where am I going?

It’s:

\boxed{\text{Who chose the vector I'm still following?}}

And the companion question:

Are they even still here?

That’s how you distinguish inheritance worth carrying from a ghost you’ve been keeping up with.